  [1mThe fork:[0m what [38;5;153muserId[39m actually [3mis[0m, and therefore whether it can be a parameter at all. Right now the submit handler
  has two values, both typed into the form. There is no third value to pass.

  - [1mIt's the canonical ID from the auth response.[0m In most apps the server tells you the real user ID [3mafter[0m it verifies
    credentials. If that's the case, [38;5;153muserId[39m is an [1moutput[0m of [38;5;153mlogin()[39m, not an input — the parameter you asked for can't be
    filled by the caller, because nobody knows the ID until login succeeds. Tracking would read it off the result.
  - [1mIt's a caller-supplied identity from elsewhere[0m — an existing session, SSO context, a tenant/account ID. Then a
    parameter is exactly right, and it flows in from whatever already knows it. Nothing in this repo produces one yet,
    so we'd be defining where it comes from.
  - [1mIt's effectively the username.[0m If username is already the unique identifier, a separate [38;5;153muserId[39m adds a second name
    for one thing, and tracking should just record [38;5;153musername[39m. No new parameter.
  - [1mIt's a client-generated device/anonymous ID[0m — a stable random ID in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m that lets you correlate failed
    attempts and pre-auth activity, not just successful logins. This one [3mis[0m available at call time and composes well
    with the other options.

  [1mMy recommendation: treat the authenticated identity as an output of [38;5;153mlogin()[39m, and add a client-generated device ID as [0m
  [1mthe actual new parameter[0m (or, more cleanly, let the tracking module own it). That gives you "who logged in" for
  successful logins via the result, [3mand[0m coverage of failed attempts — which a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter supplied by the caller
  can't give you, since a failed login has no verified user.

  This is a real departure from "add a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter," so I want your call rather than quietly redesigning around it.
